OOPS (Object Oriented Programming) is used when we want to work with data by organizing it into objects. It allows us to create our own objects that combine data and methods, and helps in abstraction, making the code more structured and easier to manage.

Bottom-up approach in OOPS means building the program by first creating small, basic objects or components, and then combining them to form more complex systems.

Opposite:
Bottom-up (OOPS) → start small → build big
Top-down → start from big system → break into smaller parts

In the top-down approach, we start from a large system, break it down into smaller components, and then work on each component step by step (structural programming).

A class is a blueprint or template used to create objects, which defines their properties and behavior.

When you create an object from a class, it inherits all the variables and functions defined inside that class.

