Compile time is the phase when the code is converted into machine code before execution.
Runtime is the phase when the program is actually executing.
Runtime polymorphism means the function call is resolved at runtime (during execution), not at compile time.
👉 Same function name → different behavior
👉 Decision happens while program is running
👉 “A virtual function is a function declared with the virtual keyword in a base class, which allows it to be overridden in a derived class and supports runtime polymorphism.”

“Runtime polymorphism is achieved using virtual functions and is typically implemented using base class pointers or references, which allows the program to decide at runtime which function to call.”
“Runtime polymorphism is usually implemented using base class pointers and virtual functions.”

